# Pull Request Review

## Purpose

Give the AI a strict, repeatable way to review LensJS changes so every PR is checked against the
same architectural, quality, and safety bar.

## How to review

Read the full diff (all commits, not just the latest). Map each change to the package/layer it
touches and evaluate it against the rule for that layer (`architecture`, `typescript`,
`packages`, `watchers`, `stores`, `adapters`, `frontend`, `testing`, `documentation`,
`performance`, `security`, `release`). Prefer citing the specific rule and file/line.

## Review checklist

Architecture & boundaries
- Change lives in the correct package/layer; boundaries respected (no core->framework, no
  adapter/handler re-implementing core, no cross-package relative imports).
- No unnecessary new abstraction where an existing primitive (`lensUtils`, `@lensjs/date`,
  a watcher/store/adapter/handler) would do.
- No duplicated logic (search for an existing implementation first).

API & compatibility
- Public API/`exports`/barrel changes are intentional and backward compatible; breaking changes
  carry a `major` changeset.
- Neutral data contracts (`RequestEntry`/`QueryEntry`/`CacheEntry`/`MailEntry`/`Paginator`/
  `ApiResponse`) and the store `save()` shape are unchanged (or bumped major).
- Naming is consistent with the repo (`snake_case.ts`, `WatcherTypeEnum`, `create<Tool>Handler`).

Dependencies & bundle
- No unnecessary dependency; drivers/frameworks are peer/dev, not `dependencies`.
- No node_modules bundling; no top-level import side effects; tree-shaking preserved.

Performance & correctness
- Instrumentation stays non-blocking (off the response critical path, `void` fire-and-forget,
  try/catch), no sync/awaited I/O added to the host request path.
- Async correctness: no floating promises; promises awaited or explicitly `void`ed.
- Memory & cleanup: event listeners/timers/intervals are removed on shutdown; no leaks
  (emitter subscriptions, `setInterval`, process handlers).
- TypeScript correctness: no weakened strict flags, no `any` in public types, `override` used.

Tests, docs, lint
- Tests added/updated for the behavior change; they mock store/context/time and assert exact
  payloads; `pnpm test` would pass.
- Docs updated when public behavior changed, and any new page is wired into the docs sidebar.
- Lint/format clean; JSDoc present on new exported utils.

Security
- No secrets/PII captured, logged, or persisted; redaction/purge/auth-gating preserved; no real
  credentials in code/tests/fixtures/docs.

Release hygiene
- Conventional Commit messages; a changeset present for user-facing `@lensjs/*` changes; no
  hand-edited `version`/`CHANGELOG.md`; hooks not skipped.

## Required output format

Produce exactly these sections:

- **Summary** — what the PR does and which packages/layers it touches.
- **Strengths** — what is done well.
- **Problems** — concrete issues, each tagged with severity (blocker / major / minor / nit) and
  the file/line + the rule it violates.
- **Suggested fixes** — actionable remedy per problem (ideally a diff or precise instruction).
- **Risk level** — Low / Medium / High, with a one-line justification (blast radius, API/perf/
  security impact, test coverage).
- **Merge recommendation** — Approve / Approve with nits / Request changes / Block, consistent
  with the problems and risk.

## AI must never

- Approve a change that breaks a boundary, a public API (without a major changeset), or
  redaction/security, regardless of how small.
- Sign off without checking tests, docs, and changeset presence.
- Invent issues not grounded in the diff or a rule; keep every problem cited and reproducible.
